// markdownlint-cli2 configuration -- the documentation quality gate for
// check_status_2107_01.
//
// PURPOSE
// This file is the machine-enforced half of the repository's Markdown style
// contract. Before it existed there was no documentation quality gate of any
// kind -- `.markdownlint.json`, `.markdownlint.yaml`, `.markdownlintrc`,
// `.prettierrc`, `.prettierignore` and `.editorconfig` were all absent -- so
// "comprehensive documentation" was a matter of reviewer taste rather than a
// pass/fail condition. Everything below replaces that subjective review with a
// single command any contributor can run:
//
//     npm run lint:md   # -> npx --yes markdownlint-cli2@0.23.2 "**/*.md"
//                       #    MUST exit 0  ("Summary: 0 issues")
//
// PINNED TOOLING
// Every decision here was calibrated against markdownlint-cli2 0.23.2, which
// bundles markdownlint 0.41.1 -- the exact pair named by the `lint:md` script
// in `package.json`. A correct run announces itself as:
//
//     markdownlint-cli2 v0.23.2 (markdownlint v0.41.1)
//     Finding: **/*.md !node_modules/** !docs-api/**
//
// That `Finding:` line doubles as proof this file was loaded. If it appears
// without the two negations, the configuration was NOT picked up and the run
// tells you nothing. Note the two different Node floors in play: this tool
// version declares `engines: node >= 22`, whereas `package.json` declares
// `node >= 18` because that is what express 5.2.1 requires. A contributor on
// Node 18 or 20 can therefore run the server but needs Node 22 or newer to run
// this gate.
//
// SCOPE DISCIPLINE
// Three rule entries, one `globs`, one `ignores` -- and nothing else. No
// plugins, no custom rules, no `fix` flag and no `outputFormatters`: each would
// add behaviour that nothing in this repository requires and would make the
// gate harder to reproduce from a clean checkout. Rules without a measured
// reason to be overridden keep their markdownlint defaults, so this file stays
// short enough to read in full before changing it.
{
  // Rule overrides. Only rules with measured evidence behind them appear here.
  "config": {
    // MD013/line-length -- DISABLED.
    //
    // Long prose lines and wide contract tables are intentional in this
    // repository, and rewrapping them would harm readability. Two concrete
    // cases force the decision:
    //
    //   1. The README's lead paragraph is a single 218-character line carried
    //      over verbatim from the tracked original. Against the rule's default
    //      80-column limit it fails as:
    //          README.md:3:81 MD013/line-length [Expected: 80; Actual: 218]
    //      Rewrapping it would rewrite prose that is deliberately preserved.
    //   2. The API reference states each endpoint's status code, exact response
    //      body, Content-Length and full response-header set in pipe tables.
    //      Those rows are wide because the contract they carry is wide, and
    //      MD060 below additionally requires their pipes to line up -- which is
    //      flatly incompatible with an 80-column ceiling.
    //
    // Disabling the rule is preferred over merely raising `line_length` to some
    // larger number: any finite ceiling would eventually be tripped by a
    // legitimately wide contract table and would invite the wrong fix, namely
    // mangling the table instead of trusting it.
    "MD013": false,

    // MD034/no-bare-urls -- ENFORCED. (This restates the markdownlint default
    // explicitly, so the choice is visible rather than inherited.)
    //
    // A bare URL is a real defect, not a stylistic quibble: it renders
    // inconsistently across Markdown processors and carries no link text, which
    // leaves readers using a screen reader with an address instead of a
    // description. Genuinely reachable external addresses must therefore use
    // standard link syntax -- for example [Express](https://expressjs.com/).
    //
    // CRITICAL INTERACTION -- do NOT satisfy this rule with an autolink.
    // The obvious response to an MD034 finding is the autolink form
    // <http://localhost:3000>, which does silence MD034 but then breaks the
    // companion link-integrity gate:
    //
    //     npx --yes markdown-link-check@3.14.2 --quiet README.md
    //
    // markdown-link-check probes every link it finds. Nothing is listening on
    // the reader's machine, so it reports
    //     ERROR: 1 dead link found in README.md !
    //     [x] http://localhost:3000 -> Status: 0
    // and exits 1. The two gates therefore only *appear* to conflict.
    //
    // The resolution, verified against both tools: write localhost and any
    // other unreachable-by-design address as an INLINE CODE SPAN. A code span
    // is not a bare URL, so MD034 passes; and it is not a link, so it is never
    // probed. Both gates then exit 0 -- which is exactly why this file needs no
    // link-check ignore list of any kind.
    "MD034": true,

    // MD060/table-column-style -- ENFORCED with style "aligned".
    //
    // The endpoint, header, configuration and repository-contents tables are
    // read as data, so their columns must line up in the raw source as well as
    // in the rendered view. "aligned" is markedly stricter than the default
    // "consistent" style, and that strictness is the entire point.
    //
    // Measured behaviour: a cell whose text is even ONE character wider or
    // narrower than the corresponding header cell produces
    //     MD060/table-column-style Table column style
    //     [Table pipe does not align with header for style "aligned"]
    // and exit 1 -- one finding per misplaced pipe. A three-column table headed
    // `| Method | Path | Response body |` with the compact delimiter row
    // `| --- | --- | --- |` and the body rows `| GET | / | Hello world |` and
    // `| GET | /good-evening | Good evening |` yields NINE findings under this
    // setting -- three on the delimiter row and three on each body row -- while
    // the identical table passes under the default "consistent" style. The
    // findings clear only once every cell is space-padded to its column width
    // so each pipe sits at an identical character offset on every line:
    //     | Method | Path          | Response body |
    //     | ------ | ------------- | ------------- |
    //     | GET    | /             | Hello world   |
    //     | GET    | /good-evening | Good evening  |
    //
    // THE CORRECT RESPONSE TO AN MD060 FINDING IS TO PAD THE OFFENDING TABLE,
    // never to relax or delete this entry. `npm run lint:md` exiting 0 is a
    // mandatory condition for this repository's documentation, not an advisory
    // one, and a gate that is weakened whenever it fires is not a gate.
    "MD060": { "style": "aligned" }
  },

  // Files to lint when markdownlint-cli2 is invoked with no glob arguments.
  // Deliberately identical to the glob in the `lint:md` script of
  // `package.json`, so that a bare `npx markdownlint-cli2` run from the
  // repository root and `npm run lint:md` examine exactly the same file set and
  // cannot disagree. The documentation is a single consolidated `README.md`
  // today; the recursive pattern keeps the gate correct without amendment if a
  // second Markdown file is ever added anywhere in the tree.
  "globs": ["**/*.md"],

  // Paths excluded from the gate. Both are git-ignored build or vendor trees,
  // and both exclusions are load-bearing rather than cosmetic:
  //
  //   node_modules/  Markdown vendored inside installed dependencies. At the
  //                  dependency set pinned by `package-lock.json` this tree
  //                  contains 116 `.md` files against exactly one of our own,
  //                  so linting it would bury this repository's findings under
  //                  upstream noise that no contributor here can fix.
  //   docs-api/      Generated JSDoc output, written by `npm run docs`. It is
  //                  build output rather than source of record, it is
  //                  regenerated on demand, and it is never committed.
  //
  // Holding this list to exactly these two entries means an exclusion can never
  // be used to hide a genuine finding in the repository's own documentation.
  "ignores": ["node_modules/**", "docs-api/**"]
}
